in @prism
in @epistemologic/property
in @beam

# @epistemologic/property/total_classification
#
# An AST is totally classified iff every byte in its source falls
# into a recognized AST node — no Dark children, no opaque body
# bytes the parser couldn't structure.
#
# A grammar that compiles without this property is honest about loss:
# the dark regions are measured, the beam carries them, the gutter
# renders them. A grammar that compiles WITH this property has been
# proved fully understood by the parser.
#
# The bootstrap walks the AST directly in Rust to enforce --strict
# (counting Dark children). The grammar body here is \ — the
# obligation closes when the bootstrap can be queried for dark
# children structurally rather than via a direct AST walk.
#
# Spec: docs/specs/strict-and-total-classification.md

grammar @epistemologic/property/total_classification {
  # walk the AST. count Dark children. return verdict accordingly.
  # pass    — zero Dark children in the AST
  # fail    — one or more Dark children present
  # partial — reserved for grammars that mix recognized and dark
  total_classification(ast) -> verdict { \ }

  # the count of dark regions in the AST. zero ⇔ verdict.pass.
  #
  # binding: the bootstrap's `count_dark` function in
  #   bootstrap/src/main.rs (commit 8e6df9c)
  # is the structural sibling of this body. It walks the AST and sums
  # nodes with kind Dark. The kintsugi formatter loop (cmd_kintsugi,
  # --shatter N) calls count_dark every tick and emits it on the beam
  # line as the cheapest loss surface — wire 5 in
  # docs/specs/kintsugi-wiring.md, the e^(n+1) < e^(n) monitor in
  # docs/specs/kintsugi-formatter.md stage 2.
  #
  # The grammar body remains `\` because the in-grammar sub-Turing
  # walk over [AstNode] is not yet declared — the AST type itself is
  # carried in the bootstrap, not yet in grammar. When `type ast` lands
  # as a structural declaration (Spec D's next tick), this body closes
  # as a fold over children counting kind == Dark. Until then the
  # bootstrap discharges the obligation; the Rust function IS the
  # witness for the `\`.
  dark_count(ast) -> u64 { \ }

  # the dark regions themselves, for diagnostics.
  # each carries the verbatim bytes and a source span (line/col
  # start + end) so the compiler can point at the unrecognized
  # construct and name the parser rule that would have closed it.
  #
  # binding: the bootstrap's `collect_dark` / `print_dark_diag` in
  # bootstrap/src/main.rs are the sibling functions. The grammar body
  # closes when `type dark_range` is declared structurally and the
  # walk is expressed as a fold.
  dark_regions(ast) -> [dark_range] { \ }
}

out total_classification
out dark_count
out dark_regions
out @epistemologic/property/total_classification
